{
 "summary": "No. A concurrency rejection is throttling, not proof that the login spent its quota. Flywheel requires a rejected usage-window event plus recognized usage-limit text to mark quota at the Claude engine boundary. The refused attempt fails through the normal adapter; the shared failover client owns retries and selection of another account or provider.",
 "findings": [
  {
   "topic": "Evidence that decides quota exhaustion",
   "claims": [
    "SDK rate-limit messages preserve status, rateLimitType, resetsAt, and billing fields before classification: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/sdk-events.ts:105-109.",
    "usageLimitRejection returns evidence only for status=rejected and a recognized usage window. The recognized set is five_hour, seven_day, seven_day_opus, seven_day_sonnet, seven_day_overage_included, and overage; concurrents is not included: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/sdk-events.ts:124-143.",
    "The usage reader replaces limitRejection with the evidence from each report, so the error map uses the last report's result: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/extra-usage-guard.ts:137-138,155-158.",
    "reportsSpentUsage requires BOTH that rejection evidence and recognized text. isUsageLimitText checks SDK message prefixes. Thus the engine's usual usage-limit text alone does not establish spent quota: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/sdk-events.ts:146-152; src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/query-launcher.ts:752-755.",
    "Only the reportsSpentUsage branch in cliFailure calls quotaFailure and markQuotaExhausted. quotaFailure creates QuotaExhaustedError carrying the mark's observation; the mark targets this candidate through build.availability: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/sdk-events.ts:193-204,243-248; src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/client.ts:118-123."
   ]
  },
  {
   "topic": "A refused request is not a paid-usage uncertainty",
   "claims": [
    "Billing classification treats a rejected report without a recognized usage window as refused, unless explicit extra-usage evidence is present: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/sdk-events.ts:71-75.",
    "The extra-usage guard records rejection evidence but returns immediately for billing=refused. It does not mark the account as extra usage or unconfirmed usage merely because the request was refused: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/extra-usage-guard.ts:137-152."
   ]
  },
  {
   "topic": "What happens to the refused engine request",
   "claims": [
    "The ordinary bridge retains the actual service answer. A 429 with anthropic-ratelimit-unified-status=rejected waits for turn-end classification; other answers fail the request immediately: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/bridge.ts:655-658; src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/service-requests.ts:232-234.",
    "At turn end, the bridge passes the error report, result, last usage rejection, and retained wire answer to cliFailure. Without spent-usage evidence, cliFailure prefers wireFailure(wire) over the engine's generic text: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/bridge.ts:259-269,394-406; src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/sdk-events.ts:193-204.",
    "wireFailure reconstructs the Anthropic error from the actual HTTP status, body, and headers, retaining the service's retry evidence: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/service-requests.ts:17-24.",
    "For a concurrency refusal, failRequest has no extended-context or engine-local login resend. It stops the query and fails the pending adapter sink with the error: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/bridge.ts:278-299,133-156.",
    "The shared Anthropic classifier maps an ordinary HTTP 429 to LlmStreamError(kind=rate_limit), including a numeric Retry-After delay. The common provider classifier maps that error to RateLimitFailure: src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/anthropic-adapter-helpers.ts:200-223; src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/provider-failure.ts:312-324.",
    "This conclusion distinguishes the actual concurrency response from genuine quota evidence in a response body. The generic quota classifier separately recognizes specified quota codes and message patterns; it does not treat every HTTP 429 as quota exhaustion: src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/errors.ts:105-126."
   ]
  },
  {
   "topic": "Shared-host requests follow the same policy, not coordinator-model hopping",
   "claims": [
    "A hosted agent's recorded service failure settles its wait with wireFailure(event.wire): src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/agent-host/hosted-requests.ts:246-258.",
    "For a failed coordinator frame, frameFailed uses the same reportsSpentUsage gate. Otherwise it uses the wire answer, and frameEnded fails each still-pending agent in the frame: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/agent-host/host.ts:363-384.",
    "Coordinator demotion accepts model refusals or HTTP 403, not an ordinary concurrency 429. The source explicitly assigns concurrency waiting to the failover client's retry, rather than changing coordinator models or marking quota: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/agent-host/host.ts:329-343.",
    "Only QuotaExhaustedError or QuotaCoordinationError triggers the frame-ended quota close path: src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/agent-host/host.ts:395-399."
   ]
  },
  {
   "topic": "Who decides retries and movement to another account or provider",
   "claims": [
    "The client factory wraps the admitted candidate list in createFailoverClient, with the availability owner and optional preferred tree login. The Claude client itself supplies operations to the common Anthropic adapter: src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/client-factory.ts:331-356; src/orchestration/modules/provider-claude-agent-sdk/client.ts:139-160.",
    "RateLimitFailure is a failover-eligible class. The assistant attempt returns it as provider_failure to the admitted request rather than settling it as a final answer: src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/provider-failure.ts:103-114; src/orchestration/agent-runtime/assistant-turn-stream.ts:239-243.",
    "createFailoverClient retries the same candidate up to three times after the initial attempt, sleeping and preparing the request again. Delay uses a carried retry hint capped at 60 seconds, otherwise exponential backoff with jitter: src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/failover-client.ts:126-146,595-599,657-665.",
    "After the retry budget is exhausted, the walk publishes the outage-shaped failure, releases probes, and selects the next candidate. Its selector walks admitted candidates, skips attempted/spent entries, and checks current readiness. This common walk, not the engine's usage-limit text, decides whether another account or provider serves: src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/failover-client.ts:421-438,667-674.",
    "A persistent provider-attributed 429 can produce a provider-level unreachable mark after all attempts, which is distinct from spending the login's quota. RateLimitFailure with an HTTP status is provider-attributed; publishOutageFailure marks the non-free provider unreachable. Quota marking is a separate QuotaExhaustedFailure branch: src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/provider-failure.ts:200-210; src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/failover-client.ts:529-543,646-655.",
    "If no admitted candidate can serve and no pending recheck remains, selection throws exhaustedChainError rather than selecting an arbitrary provider: src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/failover-client.ts:514-518."
   ]
  }
 ],
 "verification": "Read-only source trace of the supplied snapshot. No files changed, commands executed, or live provider requests made. The remote engine's actual emitted event was not observed; the conclusion applies to the concurrency event and service response described in the question, not to an uninspected production incident.",
 "files_modified": []
}

{
 "summary": "All four request kinds use the chat-tree cache group as `prompt_cache_key` when caching is enabled: root chat, its title request, compaction, and its subagent. For an OpenAI API key route, that key is in the body and both session headers carry the request's provider-session identity. For ChatGPT subscription, the body key is still populated but the backend ignores it for grouping; `session-id` and `session_id` carry the chat-tree cache group, while `x-client-request-id` carries the provider-session identity. Any of the four can opt out if resolved `cacheRetention` is `none`; then the body key is omitted, and ChatGPT headers revert to the provider-session identity (and adapter WebSocket sessionId is omitted).",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "claim": "Root chat request inputs set `providerSessionKey` from the conversation and `promptCacheKey` to the enclosing tree root, falling back to the conversation provider-session key when not in a tree. Thus the cache key's value is the chat tree root identity, not the provider-session identity, unless this is a standalone tree.",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/agent-runtime/request-inputs.ts:659-663"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Title, subagent, and related side calls share the chat tree host; child runner options inherit the enclosing run. The same request-input calculation at request-inputs.ts:659-663 therefore gives a title or subagent launched in this chat the same tree-root `promptCacheKey` and its own conversation's `providerSessionKey`.",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/agent-runtime/request-kernel.ts:23-32; src/orchestration/agent-runtime/request-inputs.ts:223-233; src/orchestration/agent-runtime/request-inputs.ts:659-663"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Compaction is a request within the current agent loop: the loop passes its run's `providerSessionKey` and `promptCacheKey` into both budget-exhaustion handling and normal turn preparation. Those values remain the chat's provider-session identity and chat-tree cache group.",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/agent-runtime/agent-loop.ts:443-448; src/orchestration/agent-runtime/agent-loop.ts:494-499; src/orchestration/agent-runtime/agent-loop-types.ts:292-298"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The normal attempt carries `cacheRetention`, `providerSessionKey`, and `promptCacheKey` into the LLM `StreamOptions`.",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/agent-runtime/assistant-turn-attempt.ts:136-153"
  },
  {
   "claim": "For either OpenAI API or ChatGPT subscription, when `cacheRetention` is not `none`, request-body construction includes `prompt_cache_key` set to the clamped `promptCacheKey` if valid. With `none`, it does not add `prompt_cache_key`; API models that accept cache options instead receive `prompt_cache_options: { mode: \"explicit\" }`.",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/openai-helpers.ts:221-236"
  },
  {
   "claim": "On the OpenAI API route, `session_id` and `x-client-request-id` both carry the clamped `providerSessionKey`. The OpenAI API's session headers do not use the prompt-cache group.",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/openai-helpers.ts:246-260"
  },
  {
   "claim": "On the ChatGPT route, when caching is enabled, both `session-id` and `session_id` carry the prompt-cache group (`promptCacheKey`, falling back to identity if invalid), while `x-client-request-id` carries `providerSessionKey`. When caching is disabled, both session headers carry identity instead. Code documents that the ChatGPT backend groups by session-id and ignores body `prompt_cache_key`.",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/openai-helpers.ts:246-260"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The ChatGPT WebSocket transport passes those same affinity headers and sends the request params as the body. When retention is `none`, it omits its separate adapter `sessionId`; otherwise that adapter field is `providerSessionKey`, distinct from the session-id cache-group header.",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/chatgpt-adapter/transport.ts:80-109"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The default retention is one hour for interactive chat and five minutes for title and subagent requests; provider cache-retention policy can replace these defaults. Therefore the opt-out is conditional, not inherently tied to a request kind or route: any of the four omits the body key if its resolved retention is `none`.",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/agent-runtime/request-kernel.ts:45-66; src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/types.ts:509-512; src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/openai-helpers.ts:222-227"
  },
  {
   "claim": "OpenAI body may also include `prompt_cache_retention` for `24h` or `in_memory` on the plain API route, but this is a retention-policy field and not the key. ChatGPT requests deliberately do not receive that field.",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/agent-runtime/llm/openai-helpers.ts:226-235"
  }
 ]
}

{
 "summary": "The person's new input wins over the restored earlier interrupt, provided the input reaches that agent and module admission permits it. An opening slot remembers the hand-back until its durable interrupt is read, so the agent runs instead of restoring that interrupt. Resume reports `resumed`, not `already_running`, on this interrupted-opening path. This is a code trace, not a live runtime test.",
 "findings": [
  {
   "claim": "A planned or capacity wake alone preserves the person's earlier interrupt.",
   "verdict": "confirmed",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/workflow-host/runner-types.ts:14-20 defines planned and capacity wakes as automatic. src/orchestration/workflow-host/agent-inputs.ts:341-350 restores a paused-by-person cut when automaticWake(wake) is true. src/orchestration/workflow-host/agent-inputs.ts:449-455 also preserves person cuts during a live capacity continuation."
  },
  {
   "claim": "There is an opening interval in which the slot exists but has not restored its durable cut.",
   "verdict": "confirmed",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/workflow-host/agent-callback.ts:276 opens agentInput. src/orchestration/workflow-host/agent-inputs.ts:337-339 creates that slot with cut UNREAD. After awaited capability planning, brief composition, projection attachment, and evidence reads, src/orchestration/workflow-host/agent-callback.ts:586-595 calls initialize."
  },
  {
   "claim": "A hand-back during that interval is retained, and initialization does not reapply the old interrupt to the addressed agent.",
   "verdict": "confirmed",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/workflow-host/agent-inputs.ts:439-447 records an authorize event on every unread slot, even when no effective cut is yet visible. src/orchestration/workflow-host/agent-inputs.ts:167-171 stores its address in handedBack, then suppresses the restored cut when any saved address reaches the initialized agent. src/orchestration/workflow-host/agent-inputs.ts:183-184 defines address matching. For already-read slots, authorize clears the cut and opening action at src/orchestration/workflow-host/agent-inputs.ts:175-179. Consequently src/orchestration/workflow-host/agent-callback.ts:637-645 starts runTurn without applying that old opening interrupt; takeOpeningInterrupt returns only the effective remaining cut at src/orchestration/workflow-host/agent-inputs.ts:378-382."
  },
  {
   "claim": "A person's admitted message follows this hand-back route even before the slot has read its cut.",
   "verdict": "confirmed",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/workflow-host/agent-inputs.ts:323-326 returns undefined evidence for an unread slot. src/orchestration/workflow-host/runner.ts:95-107 therefore still uses its durable paused evidence when determining the reached cut. src/orchestration/workflow-host/runner.ts:482-490 identifies a person-origin message and treats the interrupted target as a hand-back, subject to admission. src/orchestration/workflow-host/runner.ts:500-511 hands over held messages, delivers the new message, calls agentInputs.resume(reached), and clears the whole-cut record. Thus the resume address is retained even when direct live message delivery has not found a fully identified slot. An explicit address that does not reach the agent does not authorize it: src/orchestration/workflow-host/agent-inputs.ts:170,183-184."
  },
  {
   "claim": "Resume reports successful continuation, even if the lower-level resume count is zero because the opening cut is unread.",
   "verdict": "confirmed",
   "evidence": "src/orchestration/workflow-host/runner.ts:442-451 checks cut(address), which includes durable evidence for unread slots, then awaits resume and unconditionally returns kind continued. The lower-level boolean at src/orchestration/workflow-host/runner.ts:386-392 is pending || resumed.length > 0, but handBack does not use that boolean. src/orchestration/workflow-host/controller.ts:321-345 routes a person's Resume on the live workflow to runner.handBack and maps continued to kind resumed. src/orchestration/app-service/workflow-mutations.ts:35-41 exposes this as { ok: true }. The exception is module refusal: src/orchestration/workflow-host/runner.ts:411-417,444-445 refuses hand-back; src/orchestration/workflow-host/controller.ts:342 returns unavailable. Message refusal leaves its input held without resuming at src/orchestration/workflow-host/runner.ts:489-499."
  }
 ],
 "files_modified": []
}

